Historical Example: The Piazza d'Italia
A third major historical example of Postmodernism is the Piazza d'Italia in New Orleans, Louisiana, designed by architect Charles Moore and completed in 1978.
 Significance: It is celebrated as one of the most vibrant and radical public urban spaces of the Postmodern era, designed as an urban piazza to celebrate the city's Italian-American community.
 Characteristics: The project heavily embraces historical pastiche, irony, and theatricality. Moore combined classical Roman architectural orders (such as Doric, Ionic, and Corinthian columns) with modern, industrial materials like neon lighting, stainless steel, and precast concrete. It features a map of Italy shaped into a fountain, completely subverting serious modernist urban planning with colorful whimsy and historical quotation.
Source & Further Reading
 Architectural Reference: Charles Jencks, Post-Modernism: The New Classicism in Art and Architecture (Rizzoli, 1987) — A comprehensive study detailing how architects like Charles Moore integrated historical motifs, local culture, and playful irony into built environments.
